# systemctl — real-world examples

## Check a service's status

```sh
systemctl status sshd.service
```

## Start, stop, restart a service

```sh
sudo systemctl start plexmediaserver.service
sudo systemctl stop plexmediaserver.service
sudo systemctl restart plexmediaserver.service
```

## Reload config without a full restart

```sh
sudo systemctl reload nginx.service
```

## Enable or disable a service at boot

```sh
sudo systemctl enable plexmediaserver.service
sudo systemctl disable plexmediaserver.service
```

## Enable and start in one step

```sh
sudo systemctl enable --now pcscd.service
```

## List units and inspect dependencies

```sh
systemctl list-units --type=service
systemctl list-dependencies plexmediaserver.service
```

## Work with targets (runlevels)

```sh
systemctl get-default
sudo systemctl set-default multi-user.target   # boot to console
sudo systemctl isolate graphical.target        # switch now
```

## Command reference

| Command             | What it does                          |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| status              | show state and recent logs            |
| start / stop        | control the unit now                  |
| restart / reload    | restart, or reload config in place    |
| enable / disable    | control start-at-boot                 |
| --now               | also start/stop immediately           |
| list-dependencies   | show what a unit pulls in             |
| get-default         | show the default boot target          |
